raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Does substance use predict whom adolescents nominate, who gets nominated, or whether friends have similar use histories in American Indian (AI)-majority reservation schools, as it often does in racially heterogeneous schools?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Consequently, we expect that substance use will show weaker sender,\nreceiver, and homophily effects in AI-majority schools than observed in heterogeneous populations.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Are students especially likely to return a friendship nomination or befriend a friend’s friend in these close-knit communities? Do nominations concentrate around a few students or remain dispersed?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This cultural foundation translates the structural tendency for\nreciprocity into a normative expectation within social conduct, suggesting that reciprocal friendships may be especially\npronounced in AI adolescent networks [4].",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "we expect this tendency toward closure to be at least as strong as—and plausibly\nstronger than—that documented in less cohesive school settings, given the dense overlap of familial, school, and communal social spheres.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "We therefore explore the nature of degree effects, whether they indicate preferential\nattachment or egalitarian constraints, within these specific AI adolescent contexts.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Are depressive symptoms associated with receiving fewer nominations, initiating fewer nominations, or being friends with students who have similar symptoms?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We\ntherefore examine which of these mechanisms—receiver effects, withdrawal-related sender effects, or homophily—\noperate in AI-majority school contexts.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Where AI heritage is widely shared, does the strength of a student’s identification with that heritage still distinguish who nominates whom?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Alternatively, because AI identity is largely shared in\nthis population, its predictive power for tie formation may be limited. Given the exploratory nature of this investigation, we\ndo not formulate directional hypotheses but examine sender, receiver, and homophily effects empirically.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do established patterns of friendship within gender and grade replicate here, and does shared sexual orientation also distinguish friends?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Substantively, gender and grade homophily are among the most robust findings in adolescent network research [9, 12]; we expect\nthese patterns to replicate in AI-majority schools, where gender- and grade-segregated activities similarly organize peer\ninteraction.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Sexual orientation homophily has received limited attention in the literature, but documented social stratification along sexual identity lines in adolescent populations suggests\nthat it may likewise structure friendship ties [9].",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "In these three schools, an existing nomination, a shared friend, or a demographic similarity was a better guide to friendship patterns than substance use. Neither depressive symptoms nor strength of AI identification consistently distinguished who became friends.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Results indicated that universal structural mechanisms\n(reciprocity, transitivity) and demographic homophily (gender, grade, sexual orientation) were primary drivers of friendship.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "Similarly, depression and AI identity were not\nsubstantively associated with friendship formation. These findings reveal a distinct\nrelational ecology within AI-majority reservation schools, where peer ties are organized around structural and demographic commonalities rather than risky behaviors.",
            "page": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Prevention programs should investigate the connections that already support students in each school rather than assume that students who use substances form a distinct friendship group.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This heterogeneity carries practical implications: network-based interventions for substance use prevention may need to be calibrated to\nschool-specific relational ecologies rather than applied uniformly across reservation settings.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Gaining insight into these alternative predictors is\ncrucial for developing innovative, effective strategies for substance use prevention tailored to AI youth that may center on\nstrengths-based connections rather than social ties rooted in risky behavior.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The cross-sectional survey covered ninth and tenth graders at three deliberately different schools on one Northern Plains reservation: a small, isolated tribal school, a larger tribal school, and a private preparatory school.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In our study, we established partnerships with three schools on a large Northern Plains reservation to conduct a comprehensive cross-sectional social network survey among 9th- and 10th-grade students.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Our selection included a tribal school\nin one of the most geographically isolated and smallest villages (school A), another larger tribal school in a more populous\nvillage (school B), and a private, college preparatory school situated near the largest village in the area (school C).",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Of 263 survey respondents, representing 77% of eligible students, 247 had complete network data. The analyzed networks contained 49, 128, and 70 students respectively.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We\nhad an overall response rate of 77% across the three schools. Specifically, 72% of eligible students completed surveys in\nSchool A (n = 52 out of 72), 76% in School B (n = 140 out of 184), and 84% in School C (n = 71 out of 84), for a total sample\nsize of 263.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Of the 247 participants with complete network data, item non-response was limited to the substance use variables.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "School A (n = 49) was the smallest, located in\na geographically isolated village; School B (n = 128) was the largest, serving a more populous community; and School C\n(n = 70) was a private college-preparatory institution.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Community and youth boards reviewed the measures. Students could nominate up to ten close friends from a school roster; the outcome was a directed nomination between two responding ninth or tenth graders, whether or not the other student returned it.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "All measures were reviewed for cultural appropriateness and\nrelevance by a Community Advisory Board and a Youth Advisory Board from the participating communities prior to finalization [33].",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Students were\nasked to “select up to 10 friends you are closest with in grades 7th-12th at your school.” School rosters within the Network\nCanvas software assisted identification. For analysis, we focused on ties among responding 9th- and 10th-grade students,\nstored in a square adjacency matrix for each school.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The three substance measures distinguished ever-use from non-use, not degrees of consumption. The alcohol question required more than a sip, and the cigarette question excluded ceremonial tobacco.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Participants’ use of alcohol, tobacco, and marijuana was\nmeasured through binary variables (yes = 1; no = 0), derived from self-reported data in the survey’s initial section.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "“Have you ever had a drink (more than a sip) of alcohol, such as beer,\nwine, or hard liquor like vodka?”, “Have you ever smoked a cigarette? (Do not include ceremonial use of tobacco.)”, and\n“Have you ever used marijuana (also called weed, pot, chronic, or nug, including edibles)?”",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "When a student declined to report substance use, the researchers classified use from reports by peers who had nominated that student. Dropping these cases did not reverse the conclusions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Specifically, 16 participants (6.5%) selected ‘Refuse to answer’ for alcohol use, 14 (5.7%) for tobacco use, and 21 (8.5%)\nfor marijuana use.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "If over half of a respondent’s incoming friendship nominations (i.e., peers who had nominated them as a friend)\nindicated peer perception of them engaging in a specific substance use behavior (response ≥ 2, corresponding to ‘a few\ntimes a year’ or more), the respondent was classified as a user (1) for that variable;",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "sensitivity\nanalyses that exclude these cases entirely leave the direction of every focal effect unchanged and reverse no substantive conclusion,",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Depression was measured through the reported frequency of six CES-D symptoms rather than a clinical diagnosis.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The survey also included a measure of depression, utilizing the Center for Epidemiologic\nStudies Depression Scale (CES-D) [35–38]. This section of the survey consisted of six questions, each probing different\nfacets of depression, such as persistent sadness, feelings of failure, loneliness, and the occurrence of crying spells.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Respondents were asked to indicate the frequency of these symptoms using a scale ranging from 0 (rarely) to 3 (most or\nall of the time).",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "AI identity was a score for affirmation and belonging, calculated by averaging six items adapted with local advisers to refer to the specific Tribe.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The measurement of AI identity was assessed using six items adapted from the Affirmation and Belonging\nsubscale of the Multigroup Ethnic Identity Measure (MEIM) [39].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "For cultural relevance and specificity within this tribal community,\nitem wording was adapted in partnership with the study’s Community and Youth Advisory Boards, with generic references\nto “TRIBE” replaced by the specific tribal identifier.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "average score across these six items was computed to represent overall AI identity affirmation.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Grade came from school rosters. The models grouped reported gender as male, female, or other and sexual orientation as straight or non-straight. Demographic terms served both to describe friendship sorting and to adjust estimates for the focal attributes.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The information about each respondent’s grade level was obtained based on school rosters\nprovided by the participating schools.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "we processed gender information by male (if male is one of the selected options), female\n(if female is one of the selected options), and other (if male and female are not in the selected options).",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Given\nthe low prevalence of responses outside of the straight category, and to simplify the model, we categorized sexual\norientation as straight and non-straight.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "We therefore report the demographic terms as descriptive patterns and robustness benchmarks, and we center\nour substantive interpretation on the focal attributes.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For each school the researchers fitted a base exponential random graph model (ERGM) and three substance-specific models. This allowed them to distinguish sending, receiving, and matching on use while accounting for dependencies among friendships, including the distinction between an open two-step path and a closed triangle.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We employed Exponential Random Graph Models (ERGMs) to model the probability of friendship tie formation within\neach school, accounting for the inherent interdependence of network observations [9,24].",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "This included a base model containing structural terms (reciprocity, transitivity [GWESP], degree distributions [GWIDeg, GWODeg], and a two-path term) and core substantive variables\n(demographics, depression, AI identity). Subsequently, we estimated separate models for each substance (alcohol,\ntobacco, marijuana) that added sender (nodeofactor), receiver (nodeifactor), and homophily (nodematch) effects for the\nspecific substance use variable.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Following Snijders et al. [28], we also included a two‑path term to separate the tendency for\nopen two‑step sequences from triadic closure, thereby allowing the GWESP coefficient to more cleanly capture transitivity.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Average marginal effects put estimates on a probability scale for comparisons among the three schools. The external benchmark was published research in predominantly non-AI samples.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "AMEs represent\nthe average change in the predicted probability of a tie formation associated with a one-unit change in a predictor variable, holding all other variables constant. This provides an interpretable, population-averaged effect size that is directly\ncomparable across the three school networks [43].",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Table 1 provides a summary of prior findings for each of these attributes, organized by mechanism (sender, receiver,\nhomophily), drawn from studies of predominantly non-AI adolescent samples.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The researchers checked fit by simulating 100 networks per model. They also removed the other individual covariates to check whether adjustment had masked a substance-use association; the simpler models gave the same substantive result.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Goodness-of-fit was assessed by comparing the observed network to 100 networks simulated from each fitted model",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "In all 12 models, the observed network statistics fell within the central\nrange of simulated distributions, indicating adequate model fit.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "To assess whether the absence of substance‑use effects might be due to overcontrol (i.e., the inclusion of depression, AI identity, and demographic covariates), we estimated separate ERGMs containing only structural terms and the\nsubstance use variables (alcohol, tobacco, marijuana).",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "substance use showed no robust or consistent association with sender, receiver, or\nhomophily effects across schools, while the structural processes (reciprocity, transitivity, degree effects) remained significant.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Friendship nominations were usually mutual: reciprocity rates ranged from 93.3% to 97.7%. In every school, students were also more likely to be friends when connected through a mutual friend, beyond what the number of open two-step paths would predict.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Reciprocity rates were high across all schools (93.3–97.7%), indicating that mutual\nfriendship nominations were the norm.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Similarly, the positive coefficients for closed triads (transitivity) and negative coefficients for two‑path confirmed that triadic\nclosure—the process by which friends of friends become friends—is a fundamental organizing principle within these AI\nadolescent networks.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Sending many nominations and receiving many nominations followed different patterns. A subset of students sent disproportionately many nominations in every school, but only the private preparatory school showed a comparable concentration of popularity.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The negative and significant coefficients for geometrically\nweighted outdegree (GWODeg) across all schools indicate a centralized structure in outgoing ties, meaning a subset\nof adolescents initiated a disproportionately high number of friendship nominations.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "For incoming ties (indegree), a\nsignificant negative coefficient was observed only in School C, indicating a centralized popularity structure in that setting.\nIn Schools A and B, indegree distributions did not deviate significantly from what would be expected by chance.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Same-gender and same-grade friendships were more likely in all three schools. Students also formed ties along lines of shared sexual orientation, although that pattern was less uniform.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Gender and grade homophily were consistently\nsignificant across all schools (AME = 0.008–0.058, all p < 0.001), indicating that shared gender identity and grade level are\nprimary axes of friendship formation, as extensively documented in the broader adolescent literature [9,12].",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "Sexual orientation homophily was also significant and positive (AME = 0.002–0.028), an understudied pattern that suggests meaningful\nsocial stratification along lines of sexual identity even in these small, relatively homogeneous school settings.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Substance use did not reliably predict popularity, nomination activity, or friendship between similar users. The clearest exceptions were matching on alcohol use in School B and more nominations sent by alcohol users in School C. Marijuana matching in B was marginal; tobacco matching was absent.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "By contrast, in our AI-majority sample,\nsubstance use showed no consistent sender, receiver, or homophily effects across schools.",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "The only exceptions, alcohol\nhomophily in School B (AME = 0.0039, p < 0.01) and an alcohol sender effect in School C (AME = 0.015, p < 0.05), were isolated and did not replicate across schools or substances.",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "Marijuana use exhibited homophily in School B (AME = 0.0020, p < 0.10), while tobacco use showed no\nhomophily in any school.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Neither depressive symptoms nor AI identity affirmation meaningfully distinguished who sent or received nominations. Friends did not consistently match on identity strength, and even statistically detectable similarities in depression were very small.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Depression sender and receiver effects were substantively negligible across all schools;",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "AI identity was largely unassociated with sending or receiving ties,\nwith only a borderline positive receiver effect in School C.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "Homophily based on AI identity was\nnon-significant, and depression homophily was negligible in magnitude (statistically detectable only in Schools B and C;\nAME ≤ 0.001).",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "Three schools on one reservation cannot represent all AI communities or AI students attending racially integrated schools.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, we recognize that the results presented here represent\nonly three schools on one reservation. The history, policies, geography, and culture of this reservation all intersect with\nnetwork formation and maintenance patterns.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Our findings may not extend to AI youth in racially heterogeneous schools,\nwhere Rees, Freng, and Winfree [17] observed different network characteristics.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The contrast with non-AI research is indirect. Without a contemporary comparison group matched on school size and rural location, the study cannot establish that AI-majority and non-AI schools differ under comparable conditions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While a comparison sample of non‑AI schools\nwould strengthen generalizability, identifying a suitable dataset with comparable schools (similar size, rural location, and\ncontemporary data) was not feasible.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Therefore, we focus on providing a detailed\ndescription of network processes within this specific, understudied context, recognizing that future research is needed\nto assess whether these patterns differ across settings.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "One observation in ninth and tenth grade does not show how friendships change across developmental stages or school transitions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Furthermore, we acknowledge that these data reflect only one\ndevelopmental epoch of adolescents at one time point.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "a single, simultaneous observation\nof the network and of individual attributes cannot capture changes in network formation and behavior over time.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A cross-sectional association cannot distinguish choosing a similar friend from becoming similar after becoming friends. The estimates therefore establish neither that attributes cause friendships nor that friendships cause behavior or identity to change.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "More\nimportantly, cross-sectional ERGMs cannot analytically distinguish selection processes—adolescents choosing friends\nwith similar attributes—from influence processes—friends becoming more similar to one another over time [4,18,48].",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "not as evidence that substance use, depression, or AI identity causally affect friendship formation, nor as\nevidence that friendship networks causally shape these attributes.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The demographic coefficients describe sorting, not the independent causal effects of gender, grade, or sexual orientation. The adjustment variables were chosen for analyzing the focal predictors.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "we caution against interpreting the demographic coefficients\nas independent causal effects of demographics on tie formation: the adjustment set was chosen to support the interpretation of the focal predictors, and a single adjustment set is not generally appropriate for estimating every coefficient in the\nmodel.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Peer-based imputation could misclassify use if students project their own behavior onto friends. Because the reporters are themselves friends, it could also create or exaggerate apparent similarity within the network.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, adolescents’ perceptions of friends’ substance use can be systematically biased, including in the direction of false consensus, in which adolescents overestimate\nfriends’ engagement in behaviors they themselves perform—and perceived friend use may diverge from friends’ own\nself-reports [34].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Second, because the peers providing the perceptions are themselves embedded in the friendship network, imputed values could in principle introduce or amplify apparent behavioral similarity among connected adolescents,\nif peer perceptions are shaped by reporters’ own behavior or by friendship-group norms.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The identity finding concerns differences in affirmation within school friendships. It does not rule out an identity effect where AI students are a minority or within kinship relationships outside school.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Third, kinship networks and familial relationships—which operate largely outside the school-based friendship system we\nmeasured—may be the primary arena where AI identity shapes social ties [27].",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "Future research should examine whether AI identity effects emerge in more racially\nheterogeneous school settings, where Indigenous students constitute a minority and identity affirmation may become a\nmore salient basis for in-group solidarity.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The differences among schools suggest possible explanations involving institutional and community norms; the study does not establish which contextual factors produced those differences.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This\nschool‑level variation may reflect differences in school size, institutional environment, or local peer norms regarding\nstatus.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "Findings indicating differences in social networks, behaviors, and friendship formation across\nthe three schools also suggest the need to investigate how community contexts influence these factors.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The intervention implications are proposals for investigation, not evidence that a particular prevention strategy works in these schools.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "More qualitative research is needed to better understand AI adolescent networks and assess how\none can leverage existing patterns of friendship relations in interventions.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors extend adolescent social network analysis to a setting where AI students are the majority. They retain its distinction between network structure and individual attributes, but interpret both in light of kinship, mutual obligation, and local institutions rather than assuming that the same mechanism has the same strength everywhere.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Our analysis focused on endogenous structural factors (reciprocity, transitivity, two-path effects, and degree distributions) and individual characteristics (grade, gender, sexual orientation, depression, AI identity, and substance use).",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "We examine each attribute systematically, first summarizing evidence from non-AI adolescent populations and then articulating how the cultural context of AI-majority reservation\nschools may alter these patterns.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "In AI reservation schools, cultural values\nemphasizing kinship, collectivism, and community interdependence provide a distinct social context that likely shapes\nthese endogenous network processes, fostering a cohesive relational fabric among adolescents [26].",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Taken together, these findings suggest that while structural endogenous\nprocesses are universal, their specific parameters are calibrated by institutional context.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors revise, rather than reject, the rationale for network-based prevention. Reciprocity and friendship clustering remain useful starting points; the premise they challenge is that shared risky behavior reliably identifies the ties through which an intervention should work.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These endogenous factors are\nfundamental principles utilized in social network interventions for adolescent substance use, suggesting a promising direction for AI social network interventions [45].",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "The prevalent approach to adolescent social network interventions for substance use prevention often hinges on the presumption that friendship ties are, to some extent, founded on substance use behaviors.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Given\nthat this assumption does not align with our findings, it becomes imperative to investigate culturally relevant alternatives\nto understanding of how substance use relates to friendship formation.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Rees, Freng, and Winfree found that AI adolescents had fewer reciprocated friendships than White peers in racially heterogeneous schools. Zhang and colleagues build on that descriptive finding by modeling reciprocity and other structural processes within AI-majority schools, not by repeating the racial comparison.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "One study that did focus\nspecifically on AI youth network characteristics is Rees, Freng, and Winfree [17], who\nused Add Health to describe AI networks in heterogeneous school contexts, finding\nthat AI adolescents had fewer reciprocated friendships than White peers. However,\ntheir analysis was primarily descriptive and did not model the endogenous network\nprocesses examined here.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
